#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Knock Out Roses (Potted)", "Rosa 'Radrazz' (Double Knock Out)", "knockout_rose")[
  Knock Out Roses are exceptionally disease-resistant, repeat-blooming landscape roses producing waves of bright red blooms from June through frost. In containers, they are more versatile and moveable but require more frequent irrigation and fertilization. Potted plants use a bagged potting mix and are managed separately from the in-ground soil conditions. Note: Potted plant soil pH, nutrient levels, and drainage differ entirely from your tested garden soil.
]

=== Watering

Potted plants dry out 3–5 times faster than in-ground plants and require daily monitoring. In June–August heat: check the soil daily by pressing your finger 2 inches into the soil — water whenever it feels dry. This may mean watering every 1–2 days during hot weather. Water thoroughly until water runs freely from drainage holes. Do NOT allow pots to sit in standing water or saucers full of water. Reduce watering in September–October as temperatures drop. In fall, continue watering until the plant is moved to winter storage or until just before a hard freeze.

=== Fertilization

Container roses require more frequent fertilization than in-ground plants since nutrients leach out with every watering. Your tested garden soil conditions do NOT apply here — the bagged potting mix starts relatively neutral. Use a rose fertilizer that is relatively low in phosphorus with higher potassium — formulas like 5-1-7 or specialty rose feeds labeled for repeat bloom. Avoid standard high-P bloom boosters. Feed every 3–4 weeks from April through mid-August with a liquid rose fertilizer. Stop feeding 6 weeks before expected first frost (approximately mid-August for Commerce City). Include a liquid chelated micronutrient supplement monthly in the feeding rotation.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Knock Out Roses are self-cleaning — they do not require deadheading to rebloom, though removing spent flowers tidies appearance. In early spring (late March–April), cut the entire plant back by one-third to one-half to promote vigorous new growth and strong summer bloom. Remove any dead or crossing canes at this time. Remove rose hips in fall for a cleaner winter appearance.

=== Wildlife Value

Knock Out Roses are excellent for pollinators — the semi-double flowers provide accessible nectar for native bees, bumble bees, and butterflies throughout the long bloom season. Hummingbirds occasionally probe the open flowers. House finches may eat rose hips in fall if left on the plant. Cottontail rabbits will actively browse potted roses placed at ground level — elevate pots on stands to prevent rabbit browsing damage. Field mice are generally not attracted to rose containers. The pot environment also limits rabbit access better than in-ground planting.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=rose+black+spot+treatment")[Black spot] (circular black spots on leaves causing defoliation) is the most common rose disease — Knock Out is very resistant but not immune. Treat with fungicide containing myclobutanil or tebuconazole at the first sign. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=rose+spider+mites+treatment")[Spider mites] are especially problematic on container roses in hot, dry weather — look for stippling and fine webbing; treat with horticultural oil or miticide. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=rose+aphids+treatment")[Aphids] cluster on new growth in spring — dislodge with a strong water spray or treat with insecticidal soap. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=japanese+beetles+roses+treatment")[Japanese beetles] (bronze-green beetles skeletonizing leaves) are an increasing Front Range pest — handpick in the morning, or use neem oil; avoid Japanese beetle traps which attract more beetles than they catch.

=== Other Care

WINTER STORAGE: Container Knock Out Roses in Zone 5b need winter protection. Options: (1) Bring the pot into an unheated garage or shed before hard freeze (below 20°F) — keep barely moist through winter, do not let soil completely freeze or dry out; (2) Sink the pot in the ground in a sheltered location and mulch heavily over the top; (3) Wrap the pot in burlap or bubble wrap and place in a wind-sheltered south-facing corner. Repot every 2–3 years into a slightly larger container with fresh potting mix. Ensure pots have adequate drainage holes — drilling additional holes if needed.
